Environment variables

`ESPX_BPF_PROBE`, default `0`: Set `1` in `run_dirty_load.sh` / `run_spike_load.sh` to enable session
`ESPX_BPF_SAMPLE_RATE`, default `1`: Syscall sample rate (`10` = 1/10 events; use on laptops)
`ESPX_BPF_SLOW_US`, default `10000`: Ringbuf slow-syscall threshold (µs)
`ESPX_BPF_TARGETS`, default `tracker,nginx,redis,processor`: Roles resolved from compose
`ESPX_BPF_NATIVE`, default `0` (`1` in standalone session): Also resolve host PIDs via `pgrep` (`ESPX_BPF_NATIVE_PATTERNS`)
`ESPX_BPF_EXTRA_TARGETS`: Manual targets `pid:role:name` (comma-separated)
`ESPX_BPF_TRACK_K6`, default `1`: Discover k6 load-generator PIDs on host
`ESPX_BPF_TRACK_LOADGEN`, default `1` in k6 runs / `0` in standalone: Watch load-generator comm names
`ESPX_BPF_LOADGEN_COMM`, default `k6`: Comma-separated `/proc/comm` names for loadgen role (e.g. `k6,vegeta`)
`ESPX_BPF_DUMP_INTERVAL`, default `0` (`30` in standalone): Periodic `bpf/maps/summary.json` writes (seconds)
`ESPX_BPF_REFRESH_TARGETS`, default `0` (`30` in standalone): Re-run `bpf_resolve_targets.sh` interval (seconds)
`ESPX_BPF_METRICS_ADDR`, default empty (`:9464` in standalone): Live Prometheus exporter (`espx_bpf_connect_avg_us`, on-CPU %, runqueue p99, …)
`ESPX_BPF_TRACKER_BINARY`: Path to tracker built with `-tags espx_bpf_trace` for uprobes
`ESPX_BPF_OBJECT`, default `deploy/dev/bpf/loadtest_probe.o`: Override BPF object path
`ESPX_BPF_SUDO_PASS`: Sudo password for non-interactive probe start
`BPF_FORCE_REBUILD`, default `0`: Force rebuild `bin/bpf-collector`

BPF program (kernel)

Tracepoints attached per target PID (hash map `target_pids`):

- Syscalls — enter timestamp + exit duration; per-role histograms; optional ringbuf for slow calls
- Hot path set (always counted in report) — `epoll_wait`, `read`, `write`, `writev`, `fsync`, `fdatasync`, `connect`, `sendto`, `recvfrom`, `futex`
- Connect latency — enter/exit delta per role; `connect_avg_us` in summary and `espx_bpf_connect_avg_us` live metric
- Cgroup tracing — `cgroup_id` in `targets.json`, map `target_cgroups`; survives container PID changes when refresh is on
- Scheduler — `sched_wakeup` / `sched_switch` for runqueue latency histogram
- Network — TCP retransmit counter per connection tuple (loopback sanity)

Sampling: `config.sample_rate` in BPF; collector sets from `ESPX_BPF_SAMPLE_RATE`.

Userspace collector

`cmd/bpf-collector` responsibilities:

1. Load `targets.json` (roles: tracker, nginx, redis, processor, k6/loadgen; PID + optional `cgroup_id`)
2. Attach `loadtest_probe.o` links; refresh targets / loadgen PIDs when configured
3. Optional uprobes on `process_track` / `filter_check` when `ESPX_BPF_TRACKER_BINARY` is set
4. Poll BPF maps every 2s; merge into `summary.json` on shutdown (or periodic dump)
5. Sample `/proc/<pid>/fd`, `status`, `io` for FD/thread peaks
6. Read cgroup v2 stats for compose containers (`cpu.stat`, `memory.current`, `memory.events`)
7. Write `bpf/timeline.json` with Prometheus query template for session correlation

Roles map to Prometheus-style interpretation in `bpf-report.md` (not exported to Prometheus).

Report sections (`bpf-report.md`)

Load generator overhead: k6 `pid_stats` + RSS delta — Separate host saturation from tracker work
Scheduler / context switches: `pid_stats` — involuntary >> voluntary ⇒ CPU oversubscription
Cgroup limits: `cgroup_samples` — throttle %, memory.max events, IO
Hot syscalls: always-traced set — gnet vs Redis path on tracker
Disk durability: `write`/`writev`/`fsync`/`fdatasync` counts — group-commit coalescing gate (≥70% sync reduction)
Hot path uprobes: `process_track`, `filter_check` per campaign slot — requires `espx_bpf_trace` tracker build
File descriptors & sockets: `/proc` + BPF counters — leak detection
OS threads: `/proc` + sched fork/exit — gnet worker / thread pool growth
Syscalls (wall time): full histogram — top wall-% syscalls per role
Slow syscall events: `events.ndjson` — tail latency outliers
Network (connect latency & TCP retrans): connect avg µs + retrans per role
Interpretation: heuristics — numbered checklist in report

`analyze_bottlenecks.sh` appends a link to `bpf-report.md` and the k6 on-CPU line when the file exists.

Interpreting a laptop run

Typical healthy constrained run (2k–4k RPS, registry warm):

1. k6 on-CPU 50–85% on a 4-core laptop is normal — capacity ceiling is often the generator, not tracker Lua.
2. tracker throttle 0% — compose CPU limits not clipping (raise `cpus:` in `docker-compose.load-test.yaml` if >5%).
3. Redis read/write p99 < 10 µs on loopback — Redis is not the bottleneck.
4. tracker p99 (Prometheus) 10–50 ms with high k6 on-CPU — queueing at edge/k6, not hot-path bug.
5. Compare with abort rule: handler p99 > 80 ms for 30 s (`scripts/load-test/run_dirty_load.sh`).

When BPF and Prometheus disagree, trust BPF for host scheduling and Prometheus for application SLOs.

Prerequisites

Linux ≥ 5.8: tracepoint BPF
BTF (`/sys/kernel/btf/vmlinux`): recommended; warn-only if missing
`clang` + `libbpf` or Docker: `bpf_build.sh` fallback
`cilium/ebpf` (Go): vendored via `go.mod` in `bpf-collector`
root / CAP_BPF: attach and memlock (`rlimit.RemoveMemlock`)

Run `bash scripts/load-test/bpf_requirements.sh` before first session.

